    "source_path": "experiments/records/E004-paired-languages/packet.json",
    "source_file_sha256": "29958c8817f0d936e20582e83cdc5f13cb1e303de96dcae306c788caa48aa7ae",
    "packet_json_text": "{
  "corpus": {
    "corpus_id": "41efaae132de8b176c85b109b39ab39738708355742354d2e9714aeaf87ecb55",
    "model": "deepseek-flash",
    "origin_plan_id": "842e2fc309a1dd76503a7d275460c906571732ac69d513fc15a097998e15d417",
    "problem": "A replicated warehouse reservation snapshot is described by a capacity, logical current time, and an unordered bag of delivered records. Each record names a booking, revision, hold or release, quantity and expiry. Duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority. A higher revision supersedes lower revisions. Different records tied at a greatest revision can remain unresolved alternatives; different bookings need not share the same choice. A release or expired hold can reserve zero while an active hold reserves its quantity. Availability can be negative. People may disagree about whether uncertainty should be retained, what expiry does to obsolete records, what a criticism targets, and which source assumptions an account depends on. Explore this problem in prose. Correctness is not a condition for preserving a conjecture; false or incomplete proposals remain material for inquiry.",
    "schema": "minireason.language-corpus.v1",
    "text": "A replicated warehouse snapshot is not a single ledger but a cloud of rival stories about what has been promised. The capacity and logical time are shared coordinates, but the records arrive as a bag: orderless, possibly repeated, possibly stale, possibly contradictory. The first useful conjecture is that the snapshot's job is not to compute one timeless answer but to decide which conflicts are still alive. Duplicate delivery of the same booking, revision, kind, quantity, and expiry adds nothing; authority does not accumulate by repetition. A higher revision kills lower revisions for that booking, but only within the booking. Two bookings can disagree about which revision they have accepted without infecting each other.\n\nFor any one booking, the natural reconstruction is to group its records by revision, then keep only the greatest revision present. Within that greatest revision, a hold and a release cannot both be in force as the same fact; they are alternatives. An expired hold and a release both reserve zero. An active hold reserves its quantity. If several active holds tie at the greatest revision, they remain unresolved alternatives, not a sum and not a vote. That is a deliberate refusal: the rules do not say how to merge them, so the snapshot should retain the possibility that any one is the true one, or that none is, depending on what \"true\" is taken to mean.\n\nAvailability is then capacity minus whatever reservation is actually selected, but the word \"actually\" hides the problem. If each booking's greatest revision is unambiguous, availability may still be negative if the selected holds exceed capacity. Negative availability is not an error in the arithmetic; it is a signal that the warehouse has over-promised relative to this reconstruction. If different bookings have unresolved alternatives, availability is not a number but a set or an interval, and possibly not even a convex one. A pessimistic reading takes the maximum hold among alternatives for each booking; an optimistic reading takes the minimum. Neither is forced by the snapshot alone.\n\nNow the disagreements begin.\n\nOne disagreement is whether uncertainty should be retained at all. A reconciler might collapse each tie to the largest quantity, or to the earliest expiry, or to the record with some lexical tiebreak, and thereby produce a single availability number. That is convenient, but it manufactures authority. Another reconciler might keep all alternatives and report a range. That preserves honesty but makes downstream decisions harder. The snapshot description does not settle which is correct; it only says tied records can remain unresolved. A conjecture: the more useful representation is a partial order of possible reservations, not a set, because some alternatives may be compatible with others across bookings while others are not. A criticism: once you move to a set of possible worlds, you have smuggled in a notion of possibility that the original bag did not contain.\n\nA second disagreement concerns expiry and obsolete records. If logical current time is past a hold's expiry, that hold reserves zero. But does expiry erase the record, demote it, or merely change its effect? Suppose a booking has revision 5 expired and revision 3 active. Higher revision supersedes lower revision, so revision 5 wins, and because it is expired it reserves zero. Revision 3 is obsolete and should not be resurrected. That reading treats revision as authority and expiry as effect. Another reading treats expiry as deleting the record from the bag, after which revision 3 becomes greatest and active, so the booking reserves again. These give opposite answers. The snapshot description tilts toward the first: a higher revision supersedes lower revisions, and expiry can make a hold reserve zero. It does not say expiry removes the supersession. So the first is better supported, but the second remains a live conjecture, especially if one thinks expired records are no longer \"delivered records\" in the relevant sense. A criticism of the first: it lets a dead revision keep a live one buried forever, which may be undesirable operationally.\n\nA third disagreement is what a criticism targets. One can criticize a reconstruction rule, a particular record, a booking's history, the snapshot as a whole, or the assumption that logical time is meaningful. For example, saying \"this snapshot is inconsistent\" might mean two active holds for one booking at the same greatest revision, or it might mean total reservations exceed capacity, or it might mean the logical time is behind an expiry that someone expected to have passed. These are different claims. A conjecture: many apparent disagreements about availability are really disagreements about which target a criticism addresses. A criticism of the rule \"higher revision always supersedes\" is not the same as a criticism of a record that carries a high revision but an implausible quantity. Preserving that distinction matters because one can reject a rule while accepting the data, or accept the rule while rejecting the data as corrupt or late.\n\nA fourth disagreement concerns source assumptions. The snapshot assumes records are delivered, but not that they are complete. It assumes duplicates convey no additional authority, but not that all duplicates are identical in every field; if two records share booking and revision but differ in kind or quantity, are they duplicates or alternatives? The description says duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority, which suggests exact duplicates are inert, but near-duplicates may be unresolved alternatives. It assumes logical current time is a single coordinate, but different replicas may have different logical times; if so, expiry is replica-relative. It assumes bookings are independent, but capacity is shared, so reservations interact. It assumes a release or expired hold reserves zero, but does not say whether a release at a lower revision can cancel a hold at a higher revision. By the supersession rule, no: the higher hold wins. By a cancellation rule, maybe yes. That is a source assumption about what \"release\" means.\n\nA fifth disagreement is whether availability should be computed per booking or globally. Per booking, one can say each booking's reservation is the quantity of its greatest-revision active hold, or zero otherwise, or unresolved among tied greatest-revision active holds. Globally, availability is capacity minus the sum. But if there are unresolved alternatives, the sum is not well-defined. One can compute a lower bound by taking the minimum reservation per booking and an upper bound by taking the maximum. That gives an interval, but intervals lose correlations: booking A's high alternative might be incompatible with booking B's high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot. The snapshot does not name physical resources, so this incompatibility is invisible. A conjecture: the snapshot is under-specified for any real warehouse, and the best one can do is expose the unresolved alternatives rather than pretend to a single answer. A criticism: exposing alternatives without a policy for resolving them pushes the problem downstream, where it may be resolved badly or inconsistently.\n\nA sixth disagreement concerns negative availability. If capacity is 10 and active holds sum to 12, availability is -2. Some might call this an inconsistency and reject the snapshot. Others might call it a valid state of over-reservation. The description says availability can be negative, which suggests over-reservation is allowed. But negative availability does not say which holds are the offenders. If there are alternatives, some combinations may be negative and others not. A conjecture: the sign of availability is less important than the set of reservations that produce it, because a negative number alone does not tell you which booking to revisit. A criticism: if availability is allowed to be negative, then capacity is not a hard constraint, and the word \"capacity\" may be misleading; it is more like a soft target or a reporting threshold.\n\nA seventh disagreement is about revision gaps and missing records. Suppose a booking has revision 7 and revision 5, but no revision 6. Does revision 7 supersede revision 5? Yes, if higher revision supersedes lower. Does the absence of revision 6 matter? Not by the stated rules, but it might indicate a lost delivery. A conjecture: revision numbers are not necessarily contiguous, and treating a gap as evidence of loss is an extra assumption. A criticism: if revisions are generated in order, a gap may be meaningful, and ignoring it may hide a delivery failure.\n\nAn eighth disagreement is about tie-breaking across bookings. Different bookings need not share the same choice, so one cannot impose a global tiebreak. But a downstream consumer might want a single availability number. To get one, it must choose per booking, possibly arbitrarily. That choice is not authorized by the snapshot. A conjecture: any single-number availability is a projection that discards information, and different projections can disagree even when they respect the snapshot. A criticism: without a projection, the snapshot cannot answer the simple question \"how much is available?\" That may be acceptable if the snapshot's role is to support inquiry rather than to issue verdicts.\n\nA ninth disagreement is about time. Logical current time is given, but records have expiries. If logical time advances, expired holds become zero. Does an expired hold at a high revision still supersede a lower active hold? As above, one reading says yes. Another says expiry is a state change that makes the high revision inactive, allowing a lower revision to become relevant if it is still active. The description does not resolve this. A conjecture: the safer reading is that supersession is structural and expiry is evaluative; the high revision remains the chosen record, but its contribution is zero. A criticism: this can leave a booking with no reservation even though an older active hold exists, which may violate an operator's expectation that the warehouse still holds the goods.\n\nA tenth disagreement is about what \"delivered\" means. A record delivered to one replica may not be delivered to another. The snapshot is described as replicated, but the bag is unordered. Does the snapshot represent the union of all deliveries, or the intersection, or one replica's view? If it is a union, it may contain alternatives that no single replica has. If it is an intersection, it may omit records that matter. A conjecture: the snapshot is a merge, and merging is itself a source of uncertainty, because different replicas may have different logical times and different sets of records. A criticism: the description treats the snapshot as a single object, which may obscure the replication problem rather than solve it.\n\nFinally, there is the question of what remains material for inquiry. False or incomplete proposals are not discarded; they are part of the evidence about how people understand the system. A conjecture that a release always cancels a hold, even across revisions, may be false under the stated supersession rule, but it reveals an assumption about what releases are for. A conjecture that ties should be summed may be false under the \"unresolved alternatives\" reading, but it reveals a desire for conservatism. A criticism that the snapshot is unusable because it does not resolve ties may be substantive, but it may also be a demand for a decision that the snapshot was never meant to make. Keeping these disagreements visible is not a failure of the reconstruction; it is the reconstruction's only honest form."
  },
  "languages": {
    "lean": "```lean\n/-\n  Warehouse History Language (WHL) — a Lean-core-compatible formal language for\n  describing a replicated warehouse reservation snapshot as a bag of records.\n\n  This file declares a language, not a translation of any particular corpus.\n  It contains no quotations and no per-fragment translations.\n\n  The language is designed to be usable with Lean core alone (no Mathlib).\n  It uses only `Nat`, `List`, `Option`, `Bool`, and simple inductive types.\n  A `Bag` is represented as a `List`; bag equality is not declared, because\n  the language is about records and their relations, not about quotienting.\n\n  Primitive sorts:\n    - `Booking`  : opaque identifiers for bookings.\n    - `Revision` : natural numbers; higher is greater authority.\n    - `Quantity` : natural numbers. (Negative availability is handled separately.)\n    - `Time`     : natural numbers for logical time and expiry.\n\n  Record kinds:\n    - `hold`    : a hold of some quantity with an expiry.\n    - `release` : a release; quantity and expiry are still carried, because the\n                  language does not force a single meaning for them.\n\n  A `Record` is a quintuple (booking, revision, kind, quantity, expiry).\n  A `Snapshot` is a capacity, a logical time, and a list of records.\n\n  The language provides relations for:\n    - exact duplication (all five fields equal)\n    - revision dominance within a booking\n    - greatest revision within a booking\n    - active vs expired holds relative to logical time\n    - reservation effect of a record (zero for releases and expired holds;\n      quantity for active holds)\n    - alternative records at a greatest revision\n    - per-booking possible reservations (as sets of quantities)\n    - global availability bounds (as intervals over integers)\n\n  Conjectures, commitments and refutations are expressed as Lean `Prop`s.\n  The language does not assert which conjectures are true; it provides the\n  vocabulary to state them and to record refutations as proofs of negation\n  or as explicit counterexamples.\n\n  This construction permits: precise statements about which records remain\n  live under a given reading of supersession; separation of structural\n  supersession from evaluative expiry; explicit representation of unresolved\n  alternatives as sets of quantities; and formal criticism of rules by\n  stating alternative rules and deriving different results.\n\n  It may obstruct: reasoning about replication as such, because the snapshot\n  is a single list and the language has no primitive for multiple replicas;\n  reasoning about physical resource sharing beyond capacity, because capacity\n  is the only global constraint; and reasoning about the provenance or\n  authority of deliveries, because delivery is not modelled.\n-/\n\n/-! ## Primitive sorts -/\n\naxiom Booking : Type\naxiom Revision : Type\naxiom Quantity : Type\naxiom Time : Type\n\naxiom revision_le : Revision → Revision → Prop\naxiom revision_lt : Revision → Revision → Prop\naxiom revision_le_refl : ∀ r, revision_le r r\naxiom revision_lt_iff : ∀ r s, revision_lt r s ↔ revision_le r s ∧ ¬ revision_le s r\n\n/-! ## Record kinds and records -/\n\ninductive Kind : Type\n| hold : Kind\n| release : Kind\n\nstructure Record : Type := \n  (booking : Booking)\n  (revision : Revision)\n  (kind : Kind)\n  (quantity : Quantity)\n  (expiry : Time)\n\n/-! ## Snapshot -/\n\nstructure Snapshot : Type :=\n  (capacity : Quantity)\n  (logical_time : Time)\n  (records : List Record)\n\n/-! ## Basic relations on records -/\n\ndef exact_duplicate (r s : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.booking = s.booking ∧\n  r.revision = s.revision ∧\n  r.kind = s.kind ∧\n  r.quantity = s.quantity ∧\n  r.expiry = s.expiry\n\n/-- `s` has greater revision than `r` for the same booking. -/\ndef higher_revision (r s : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.booking = s.booking ∧ revision_lt r.revision s.revision\n\n/-- `r` is dominated within its booking by some record in the list. -/\ndef dominated_in (rs : List Record) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  ∃ s, s ∈ rs ∧ higher_revision r s\n\n/-- `r` has greatest revision among records of its booking in `rs`. -/\ndef greatest_revision_in (rs : List Record) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  r ∈ rs ∧ ¬ dominated_in rs r\n\n/-- Active hold relative to logical time: kind is hold and logical time has not\n    passed expiry. -/\ndef active_hold (t : Time) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.kind = Kind.hold ∧ ¬ (t > r.expiry)\n\n/-- Expired hold relative to logical time. -/\ndef expired_hold (t : Time) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.kind = Kind.hold ∧ t > r.expiry\n\n/-- Reservation effect under the \"structural supersession, evaluative expiry\" reading. -/\ndef reserves_effect (t : Time) (r : Record) : Quantity :=\n  match r.kind with\n  | Kind.hold => if t > r.expiry then 0 else r.quantity\n  | Kind.release => 0\n\n/-! ## Alternatives at greatest revision -/\n\n/-- Two records are alternatives at a greatest revision for a booking if they\n    are both in the snapshot, both have the greatest revision for that booking,\n    and they are not exact duplicates. -/\ndef alternatives_at_greatest (rs : List Record) (r s : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.booking = s.booking ∧\n  greatest_revision_in rs r ∧\n  greatest_revision_in rs s ∧\n  ¬ exact_duplicate r s\n\n/-- The set of quantities that a booking could reserve under the unresolved-alternatives\n    reading, given a snapshot. Represented as a list of quantities (may contain duplicates).\n    For each record at the greatest revision for the booking, include its reservation effect.\n    If no such record exists, the list is empty (booking reserves nothing). -/\ndef possible_reservations (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : List Quantity :=\n  let rs := snap.records;\n  let t := snap.logical_time;\n  (rs.filter (λ r, r.booking = b ∧ greatest_revision_in rs r)).map (reserves_effect t)\n\n/-! ## Availability bounds -/\n\n/-- Unary minus for natural numbers encoded as integers, to allow negative availability. -/\ninductive Int : Type\n| of_nat : Nat → Int\n| neg_succ_of_nat : Nat → Int\n\nnamespace Int\ndef sub_nat (a b : Nat) : Int :=\n  if a ≥ b then Int.of_nat (a - b) else Int.neg_succ_of_nat (b - a - 1)\nend Int\n\n/-- Minimum quantity in a list, if any. -/\ndef list_min? : List Quantity → Option Quantity\n| [] => none\n| (x :: xs) => some (xs.foldl (λ acc y, if y < acc then y else acc) x)\n\n/-- Maximum quantity in a list, if any. -/\ndef list_max? : List Quantity → Option Quantity\n| [] => none\n| (x :: xs) => some (xs.foldl (λ acc y, if y > acc then y else acc) x)\n\n/-- Per-booking lower bound: minimum possible reservation, or 0 if none. -/\ndef booking_lower_bound (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : Quantity :=\n  match list_min? (possible_reservations snap b) with\n  | none => 0\n  | some q => q\n\n/-- Per-booking upper bound: maximum possible reservation, or 0 if none. -/\ndef booking_upper_bound (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : Quantity :=\n  match list_max? (possible_reservations snap b) with\n  | none => 0\n  | some q => q\n\n/-- Global lower availability bound: capacity minus sum of per-booking lower bounds. -/\ndef availability_lower_bound (snap : Snapshot) (bookings : List Booking) : Int :=\n  let total_lower := bookings.foldl (λ acc b, acc + booking_lower_bound snap b) 0;\n  Int.sub_nat snap.capacity total_lower\n\n/-- Global upper availability bound: capacity minus sum of per-booking upper bounds. -/\ndef availability_upper_bound (snap : Snapshot) (bookings : List Booking) : Int :=\n  let total_upper := bookings.foldl (λ acc b, acc + booking_upper_bound snap b) 0;\n  Int.sub_nat snap.capacity total_upper\n\n/-! ## Conjectures, commitments, refutations -/\n\n/-- A conjecture is a proposition about a snapshot. -/\ndef Conjecture : Type := Snapshot → Prop\n\n/-- A commitment is a proposition that a reasoner endorses. -/\ndef Commitment : Type := Prop\n\n/-- A refutation of a conjecture is a snapshot together with a proof that the\n    conjecture does not hold for that snapshot. -/\nstructure Refutation (c : Conjecture) : Type :=\n  (snap : Snapshot)\n  (not_c : ¬ c snap)\n\n/-- Example schema: a rule states a reservation function for a single record. -/\ndef ReservationRule : Type := Time → Record → Quantity\n\n/-- Two rules disagree on a snapshot if they assign different possible reservations\n    to some booking. -/\ndef rules_disagree (r1 r2 : ReservationRule) (snap : Snapshot) : Prop :=\n  ∃ b, possible_reservations_with r1 snap b ≠ possible_reservations_with r2 snap b\n\n/-- Reservation possibilities parameterized by a rule. -/\ndef possible_reservations_with (rule : ReservationRule) (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : List Quantity :=\n  let rs := snap.records;\n  let t := snap.logical_time;\n  (rs.filter (λ r, r.booking = b ∧ greatest_revision_in rs r)).map (rule t)\n\n/-- Alternative rule: expiry deletes the record, so a lower revision can become greatest. -/\ndef expired_deletes_rule (t : Time) (r : Record) : Quantity :=\n  match r.kind with\n  | Kind.hold => if t > r.expiry then 0 else r.quantity\n  | Kind.release => 0\n\n/-- The standard rule and the deletion rule can differ in which records are\n    considered greatest. This is stated as a possible disagreement, not resolved. -/\ndef deletion_changes_greatest (snap : Snapshot) : Prop :=\n  ∃ b r, greatest_revision_in snap.records r ∧\n         r.booking = b ∧\n         expired_hold snap.logical_time r ∧\n         ∃ s, s ∈ snap.records ∧ s.booking = b ∧\n              revision_lt s.revision r.revision ∧\n              active_hold snap.logical_time s\n\n/-! ## Notes on intended meaning -/\n\n/- \n  The language treats the snapshot as a bag of records with no order.\n  Order is not primitive; membership and filtering are the only structural\n  operations.\n\n  \"Duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority\" is represented by\n  `exact_duplicate`: two records with all five fields equal are inert with\n  respect to each other. The language does not say that near-duplicates are\n  inert; they may be alternatives.\n\n  \"A higher revision supersedes lower revisions\" is represented by\n  `greater_revision_in`, which ignores lower revisions for the same booking.\n  It does not cross booking boundaries, because `higher_revision` requires\n  equality of booking.\n\n  \"A release or expired hold can reserve zero while an active hold reserves its\n  quantity\" is represented by `reserves_effect`. This is one reading; the\n  alternative reading where expiry deletes the record is represented by\n  `expired_deletes_rule`, but the language does not choose between them.\n\n  \"Availability can be negative\" is represented by `Int.sub_nat`, which yields\n  an integer that can be negative.\n\n  \"Unresolved alternatives\" is represented by `possible_reservations`, which\n  is a list of quantities, not a single quantity. Different bookings have\n  separate lists; there is no global choice function.\n\n  \"Different bookings need not share the same choice\" is represented by the\n  absence of any mechanism that forces a single choice across bookings.\n\n  \"What a criticism targets\" is represented by the separation between\n  conjectures about rules (`ReservationRule`) and conjectures about data\n  (`Record` and `Snapshot`). A refutation targets a conjecture by exhibiting\n  a snapshot for which it fails.\n\n  \"Source assumptions\" are not primitive. The language can state them as\n  conjectures or commitments, but it does not encode a notion of assumption\n  dependence. This is an expressive limitation.\n\n  \"Replication\" is not primitive. The snapshot is a single list. The language\n  cannot distinguish union, intersection, or replica-relative views. This is\n  an expressive limitation.\n\n  \"Physical resource sharing\" is not primitive beyond capacity. The language\n  cannot express incompatibility between alternatives of different bookings\n  except through the capacity sum. This is an expressive limitation.\n\n  \"Logical time per replica\" is not primitive. There is one logical time in\n  the snapshot. The language can state conjectures about replica-relative\n  time but cannot model multiple times directly. This is an expressive\n  limitation.\n\n  The language does not assert that any conjecture is true. It provides\n  vocabulary for stating conjectures, commitments, and refutations.\n-/\n```",
    "nonlean": "Non-Lean Semantic Class: **Rival-Story Semantics (RSS)**\n\nRSS is a non-Lean, noncomputable semantic class for the frozen warehouse prose. It is not a proof assistant language. It is a way of assigning meanings to statements about a replicated warehouse snapshot by treating the snapshot as a collection of rival stories rather than as a database. It is deliberately noncomputable: it does not provide an algorithm for resolving conflicts, and it treats any demand for a single answer as a projection that must be justified outside the semantics.\n\n**Primitives**\n\n1. **Story**: a story is a claim about what is promised for a booking. A story is not a record. Records are evidence for stories. A story has a booking, a revision, a kind (hold or release), a quantity, an expiry, and a provenance (which delivery or set of deliveries it is drawn from). Provenance is not ordered; it is a set of delivery events.\n\n2. **Bag**: an unordered collection of deliveries. A delivery is an event that makes a record available. Deliveries are not ordered, may be repeated, and may differ in logical time if they come from different replicas. The bag is the raw material, not the story.\n\n3. **Coordinate**: a capacity and a logical time. A coordinate can be shared or replica-relative. RSS treats coordinates as parameters of a viewpoint, not as absolute facts.\n\n4. **Viewpoint**: a pair (coordinate, bag). A viewpoint is what a reasoner sees. Different viewpoints may disagree about logical time, capacity, and which deliveries are present.\n\n5. **Conflict**: a situation in which two or more stories for the same booking at the same greatest revision are not exact duplicates. Conflict is not error; it is material.\n\n6. **Liveness**: a story is live under a viewpoint if it is not superseded by a higher revision for the same booking within that viewpoint, and if its kind and expiry are considered under the viewpoint's reading of expiry.\n\n7. **Reading**: a reading is a policy that decides how to treat expiry, releases, duplicates, and ties. Readings are not part of the snapshot; they are supplied by reasoners. RSS does not choose a reading. It compares readings.\n\n8. **Projection**: a function from a viewpoint and a reading to a single answer (for example, a single availability number). Projections are allowed but not privileged. A projection is always a loss of information.\n\n**Relations**\n\n- **Exact duplication**: two deliveries are exact duplicates if all record fields and provenance are equal. Exact duplicates add no authority.\n- **Near-duplication**: two deliveries share booking and revision but differ in kind, quantity, or expiry. Near-duplicates are candidates for conflict. RSS does not decide whether they are duplicates or alternatives; it records the disagreement.\n- **Supersession**: for a given booking, a story with higher revision supersedes a story with lower revision. Supersession is structural within a reading; whether it is structural across readings is a matter of conjecture.\n- **Expiry effect**: under one reading, an expired hold reserves zero but remains superseding; under another, an expired hold is deleted and lower revisions can become live. RSS represents both as readings, not as facts.\n- **Release effect**: under one reading, a release at a lower revision cannot cancel a hold at a higher revision; under another, cancellation is possible. RSS represents both as readings.\n- **Alternative compatibility**: two stories from different bookings may be compatible or incompatible depending on unstated physical resources. RSS marks this as invisible unless a viewpoint includes a resource model.\n- **Negative availability**: availability is a derived quantity that can be negative under a reading. RSS treats the sign as a signal, not an error, and treats the set of reservations as more informative than the sign.\n\n**Combinations**\n\nRSS combines primitives into **accounts**. An account is a viewpoint, a reading, and a set of accepted conjectures. Accounts are not merged. Disagreement between accounts is a first-class object. An account can be criticized by another account, and the criticism targets a specific element: a record, a rule, a booking history, a snapshot, a coordinate, or an assumption.\n\nRSS also allows **inquiry states**. An inquiry state is a collection of accounts plus a set of open questions. Open questions are not solved by RSS; they are carried forward. A conjecture is open if no account has committed to it or refuted it. A commitment is an account's acceptance of a conjecture. A refutation is an account's production of a counterexample or a derivation of inconsistency within its own reading.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nA statement in the frozen prose is interpreted in RSS by locating its primitives and relations. For example, \"duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority\" is interpreted as: exact duplicates are inert in all readings; near-duplicates are not covered by this statement and remain candidates for conflict. \"A higher revision supersedes lower revisions\" is interpreted as: within a booking, supersession is structural under all readings; across bookings, it has no force. \"Availability can be negative\" is interpreted as: under readings that select reservations, derived availability may be negative; RSS does not treat this as an error. \"People may disagree about whether uncertainty should be retained\" is interpreted as: different accounts may adopt different readings, and RSS does not rank them.\n\nRSS interpretation is noncomputable because it does not specify an algorithm for selecting a reading or resolving conflicts. It specifies what would count as an interpretation, and it allows multiple interpretations to coexist.\n\n**Conjectures, commitments, refutations**\n\n- A **conjecture** is a proposition about an account or a class of accounts. It may be about a reading, a rule, a record, a snapshot, or an assumption. Conjectures are not 